Q: What main “feature” of natural languages prevents their use (so far) as programming languages, and why?
A: From what I read, the main feature of natural languages that prevents their use as programming languages is ambiguity. In natural languages many words have multiple meanings and are meant to be interpreted in many ways, but in programming, it is crucial that there is no ambiguity to ensure that the code does exactly what it is intended to do.

